Chert-Hymnal

Ingredients: BLUEGRASS + NEW AGE + DARK, SINISTER & GOTHIC + Cello
Creative Catalyst: It emerged from a desert caravan culture that trades in salvaged synthesizers rewired to draw power from dry heat and dead truck batteries.

Chert-Hymnal sounds like an Appalachian prayer meeting dragged through thousands of miles of parched dunes, stripped of optimism, and welded to catastrophic low frequencies. Frantic, high-velocity five-string banjo clawhammering and weeping microtonal mountain modalism collide violently with deep, cathedral-scale bow drags on detuned cellos, while fried analog synthesizers exhale immense, tranquil, yet deeply unsettling ambient pads underneath.

Genre DNA
- Tempo & Pulse: 60–78 BPM half-time pulse anchored by lumbering, ritualistic foot-stomp thuds, contrasting against frenzied 16th-note banjo rolls and mandolin tremolos oscillating at twice the perceived speed.
- Harmonic Palette: Appalachian Dorian and Phrygian dominant scales steeped in droning open-fifth double stops, unresolved minor seconds, and eerie, wind-drifted New Age microtonal tunings.
- Production Signatures: "Alkali Ducking," where cavernous, dust-damaged Oberheim and Prophet synthesizer drones violently duck beneath the percussive click of gut banjo picks; "Cistern Reverb," capturing the acoustic resonance of empty underground water cisterns; and extreme close-mic placement on cello f-holes, capturing the rasp of rosined horsehair and splitting cedarwood.
- Dynamics: Glacial, meditative soundscapes dominated by breathy flute-synths and crystalline wind chimes that suddenly collapse without warning into suffocating, high-decibel downstrokes of overdriven cello and frenetic, clawhammer pick-frenzy.

Scene & Origin Lore
The genre ignited along the cracked salt pans of the Dead Meridian, an expanse traversed by the Silt-Pike Caravans—nomadic salvagers who scour abandoned military outposts and dry shipping terminals for pre-collapse synthesizers. Living beneath tarpaulins woven from hemp and silver solar-foil, these scavengers strip charred circuit boards and graft them into five-string banjos, mountain dulcimers, and acoustic cellos. Their evening gatherings are liturgical trades: a patch cable is bartered for a sheep carcass; an intact Moog filter is traded for fresh well water. Performers, clad in dust-cloaks stiffened with tallow and wearing goggles fitted with green CRT glass, perform seated around iron fire-pits, singing antique gospel refrains inverted into dark fatalist chants while their hand-wired synth arrays whistle with sand-static.

Typical Instruments
- Lead & Bass Voice: 5-String Acoustic/Electric Cello (bowed with horsehair dressed in powdered red glass; run through rat-distortion and octave pedals)
- High-Velocity Strings: Open-back Gut Banjo, archtop mandolin, and hammered dulcimer
- Salvaged Electronics: Patch-corded Korg MS-20s, scorched Prophet-5 modules, and circuit-bent FM synthesizers re-soldered to track desert wind drift
- Atmospheric & Percussion: Resonant iron-filing meditation bowls, foot-thumped cedar crates, and wind chimes cast from brass shell casings

For Fans Of: Current 93, Ben Frost, 16 Horsepower, Wovenhand

